.TH PODLAZD 8 "August 2026" "podlaz" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
podlazd \- local podlaz daemon for proxy and VPN lifecycle state
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY podlazd
.YS
.PP
Packaged systemd deployments normally enable and start the daemon during package
configuration. Administrator recovery commands are:
.EX
sudo systemctl enable --now podlazd
systemctl status podlazd --no-pager
journalctl -u podlazd -n 50 --no-pager
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B podlazd
is the local daemon for podlaz. It owns daemon runtime state, the local
Unix socket API, generated runtime configuration, supervised Xray child
processes, startup recovery scan visibility, and daemon-owned cleanup or
networking transactions where the current milestone enables them.
.PP
The daemon API is local-only and uses HTTP/JSON over Unix domain sockets.
Packaged deployments keep the filesystem socket at
.IR /run/podlaz/podlazd.sock
as an internal/admin fallback and expose a packaged abstract Unix socket for
ordinary CLI clients that must be authorized through polkit. User-facing
command names, output, exit codes, and JSON shape belong to
.BR podlaz (1)
and the canonical project documentation.
.PP
The packaged service is the privileged host-networking boundary for TUN mode.
It runs the daemon as UID 0 with a restricted capability bounding set for
route, nftables, ownership, and child-identity transitions. Proxy-only Xray runs
as the dedicated unprivileged
.BR podlaz-xray:podlaz-xray
identity without network-administration ambient capability. Native TUN Xray runs
as the same dedicated identity with only
.B CAP_NET_ADMIN
ambient capability so it can own packet ingestion and link setup through the
native Xray
.B tun
inbound.
.SH SERVICE MANAGEMENT
The packaged service unit is
.BR podlazd.service .
The Debian package normally makes this service available for first-run CLI use
through package maintainer scripts. If service recovery is needed, typical
administrator commands are:
.EX
sudo systemctl enable --now podlazd
systemctl status podlazd --no-pager
journalctl -u podlazd -n 50 --no-pager
sudo systemctl stop podlazd
.EE
.PP
A systemd restart is a continuation boundary, not an implicit user disconnect.
The packaged unit uses
.B RestartKillSignal=SIGUSR1
so podlazd preserves the current-boot reconnect intent while performing its
normal exact transaction-backed teardown. An explicit service stop uses
.B KillSignal=SIGTERM;
podlazd removes reconnect intent before teardown, so a later manual start stays
disconnected. Normal reconnect intent is stored only under
.I /run
and is bound to the Linux boot ID, so it does not authorize reconnect after a
machine reboot.
.PP
The service uses
.B KillMode=mixed
so the initial stop or restart signal goes to the daemon main process before
systemd force-terminates any remaining processes. This gives podlazd the first
opportunity to order host-network rollback and supervised Xray shutdown.
.B RuntimeDirectoryPreserve=yes
keeps exact recovery evidence available after incomplete teardown, while
.B TimeoutStopSec
is longer than the daemon's bounded rollback and core-stop budget.
.PP
Packaged service access for ordinary users is polkit-mediated by default. The
unit sets
.B PODLAZ_SERVICE=systemd
and
.BR PODLAZ_POLKIT_AUTHORIZATION=required ,
so normal commands such as
.BR "podlaz status" ,
.BR "podlaz connect" ,
.BR "podlaz disconnect" ,
and
.BR "podlaz recover --execute --yes"
should not require adding the current user to the
.B podlaz
group. If authentication is required, the system polkit agent prompts for an
administrator credential.
.PP
After manually installing the repository unit for development, reload systemd
before starting the service:
.EX
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemd-analyze verify /etc/systemd/system/podlazd.service
sudo systemctl start podlazd
podlaz status
.EE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/bin/podlazd
Packaged daemon path referenced by
.BR podlazd.service .
.PP
Local development can run
.BR "go run ./cmd/podlazd"
or manually install a development binary, but those development paths are not
the default packaged daemon path.
.TP
.I /run/podlaz/
Daemon runtime directory. Packaged systemd units create it with
.B RuntimeDirectory=podlaz ,
mode
.BR 0711 ,
and
.BR RuntimeDirectoryPreserve=yes .
The execute bits allow traversal for the daemon socket boundary and for the
unprivileged
.B podlaz-xray
child identity to reach generated runtime config when required. They do not make
daemon-private runtime state listable or writable by ordinary users. Runtime
preservation keeps same-boot exact recovery evidence across service replacement
or incomplete stop; the directory remains volatile and is not a reboot-persistent
connection source of truth.
.TP
.I /run/podlaz/podlazd.sock
Filesystem local daemon API socket. The daemon applies socket mode
.BR 0660 .
Packaged CLI clients use the polkit-gated abstract socket after filesystem
permission denial; this filesystem socket remains a narrow internal/admin
fallback rather than the normal user-facing access path.
.TP
.I /usr/share/polkit-1/actions/io.github.aidarkhusainov.podlaz.policy
Packaged polkit action policy for privileged daemon operations.
.TP
.I /run/podlaz/generated/
Daemon-owned generated runtime configuration directory. Generated Xray config
files are volatile runtime output, not persistent source of truth, and are
made readable only to the dedicated child group when the daemon must drop
child process credentials.
.TP
.I /run/podlaz/transactions/
Volatile transaction state used by status, diagnostics, rollback, disconnect,
and recovery paths. Transaction files remain daemon-private state. Exact
transaction ownership is the cleanup authority for surviving Podlaz networking
state; reconnect intent does not grant cleanup authority. Terminal
cleanup-complete history may remain for inspection but must not block a new TUN
transaction.
.TP
.I /run/podlaz/network-session-continuation.json
Daemon-private, mode-0600, atomically replaced current-boot reconnect intent.
It contains the exact connect request required to resume an active session and
may therefore contain profile credentials or endpoints. It must not be logged,
does not grant host-network cleanup authority, is removed before explicit
disconnect or service stop, and is rejected when its boot ID does not match the
current boot.
.TP
.I /run/podlaz/legacy-upgrade-continuation
Mode-0600 current-boot compatibility marker used only when upgrading from a
release that predates normal continuation records. The marker contains only the
boot ID and grants neither profile identity nor cleanup authority. The new daemon
accepts reconstruction only from one exact committed TUN transaction and its
transaction-owned generated Xray configuration, with canonical config equality
required before reconnect intent is persisted.
.TP
.I /run/podlaz/diagnostics/tun-last.json
Daemon-owned latest TUN diagnostic report. It is centrally redacted, bounded,
atomically replaced, and stored with mode 0600. Failed-connect and terminal
revalidation history may remain readable after rollback and daemon restart for
the lifetime of
.IR /run .
.TP
.I /var/lib/podlaz/
Reserved daemon state directory created by
.BR StateDirectory=podlaz
with mode
.BR 0700 .
The current packaged baseline does not use it as profile or subscription
source of truth.
.TP
.I packaging/systemd/podlazd.service
Repository systemd unit source.
.TP
.I packaging/sysusers.d/podlaz.conf
Repository sysusers definition for packaged service identities.
.SH SECURITY MODEL
The packaged daemon runs as
.BR root:podlaz
because TUN mode must inspect and mutate host routes, DNS, and nftables state
inside daemon-owned transactions around the Xray-owned TUN link. The unit
restricts the daemon with
.BR CapabilityBoundingSet=CAP_CHOWN CAP_SETUID CAP_SETGID CAP_KILL CAP_NET_ADMIN
and keeps
.BR AmbientCapabilities=CAP_SETUID CAP_KILL CAP_NET_ADMIN .
.PP
The daemon must not run Xray as UID 0. When the packaged daemon is UID 0, it
starts Xray as the dedicated
.BR podlaz-xray:podlaz-xray
identity with supplementary groups disabled. Proxy-only Xray receives no
network-administration ambient capability. Native TUN Xray receives only
.B CAP_NET_ADMIN
as ambient capability because pinned Xray must open
.I /dev/net/tun
and configure the Xray-owned TUN link. Generated Xray runtime configuration
remains private by using ownership
.BR root:podlaz-xray ,
generated directory mode
.BR 0750 ,
and generated config file mode
.BR 0640 .
.PP
Proxy-only lifecycle must not mutate TUN devices, routes, policy rules, DNS
settings, nftables, or firewall state. A new TUN lifecycle first selects one
collision-free Network Session allocation from authoritative read-only IPv4
address, numeric route-table, and numeric policy-rule evidence. Historical
address, table, or priority values are preferred candidates only and never prove
ownership. The daemon may mutate only the exact allocated address, routes,
policy rules, DNS, and nftables resources represented in its transaction state
and durable rollback ownership. A pre-existing exact server-bootstrap host route
may be persisted as a read-only prerequisite and reverified without becoming
rollback authority. Unrelated TUN devices, NetworkManager VPN connections,
routes, policy rules, DNS links, and firewall objects remain baseline state and
are not stopped, deleted, or adopted merely because they resemble Podlaz state.
Xray-owned
.B podlaz0
is released by stopping the Xray child, not by recording it as a podlaz-owned
TUN rollback target. The CLI must not become a privileged or SUID networking
mutator.
.PP
The verification phase of current-health revalidation is read-only and grants
no new mutation or repair authority. Durable
.B desired_plan
may be reconstructed to verify the complete intended composition, including
prerequisites that pre-existed connect, but cleanup remains limited to exact
recorded rollback ownership. Ambiguous or incomplete observation fails closed.
The canonical DNS verifier requires the exact planned DNS-server set, exact
route-only domain set, and default-route evidence on the owned link. The
canonical nftables verifier requires the exact owned table composition,
including chain cardinality/name/type/hook/numeric priority/policy and ordered
rule cardinality/content; extra or foreign state inside the owned table is not
accepted as healthy.
.PP
A proved active-session verification failure or revalidation deadline has a
separate fail-safe disposition. After the read-only verifier releases
revalidation authority, podlazd may persist bounded redacted pre-cleanup
diagnostics and invoke only the normal exact transaction-backed lifecycle
.B Disconnect
path. This does not expand rollback ownership and is not a repair mechanism.
Explicit user disconnect/recovery or daemon-shutdown cancellation already owns
cleanup and therefore does not schedule a second automatic disconnect.
.SH RUNTIME STATE
On startup, the daemon creates or uses the runtime directory and acquires an
advisory kernel lock on the daemon lock file. A stale lock-file inode alone does
not block crash restart; only a live kernel lock does. Before normal lifecycle
mutations can proceed, the daemon loads current-boot continuation intent,
converges exact transaction-backed recovery, and replays the saved connect
request only after recovery succeeds. If exact recovery or replay remains
incomplete, status and recovery stay available while conflicting connect and
disconnect mutations remain blocked. The daemon then removes only a stale Unix
socket at the filesystem socket path, fails if that path exists but is not a
Unix socket, listens on the filesystem and packaged abstract sockets as
configured, and serves the local API.
.PP
During proxy-only connect, the daemon selects the Xray execution identity,
validates the mode and profile snapshot received from the CLI, builds a
generated Xray config, writes it under
.IR /run/podlaz/generated/ ,
starts Xray as a supervised child process, and records active state for
status. Proxy-only lifecycle must not mutate TUN devices, routes, policy
rules, DNS settings, nftables, or firewall state.
.PP
During TUN connect, the daemon verifies native Xray TUN support, collects the
current host baseline, and proves a concrete server bootstrap path before host
mutation. It obtains canonical numeric routing evidence, deterministically
selects a free session IPv4 /32, routing-table ID, and ordered policy-rule
priorities, and persists those exact desired identities before supervised Xray
start or host-network mutation. Historical
.BR 198.18.0.1/32 ,
table
.BR 51820 ,
and priorities
.B 9999/10000
are candidates only; occupied candidates cause deterministic reallocation. Xray
then creates the TUN link, and podlazd binds its exact name, ifindex, and TUN
type before assigning the allocated address. An exact pre-existing main-table
host route that already provides the planned server bootstrap path is verified
and retained as an unowned prerequisite; otherwise the session may add the
exact host route and records ownership only if it actually mutates that route.
The daemon applies the remaining exact session-owned routes, policy rules, DNS,
and nftables state, verifies connectivity, and commits only after those checks
pass. A foreign object that races into an allocated identity causes apply to
fail rather than being adopted. Xray remains the only owner of link creation and
lifetime. Resolved DNS verification requires the exact planned DNS-server set,
exact route-only domain
.B ~.,
and DNS default-route evidence; extra DNS servers or route-only domains on the
owned link fail verification. The nftables verification used by the same
canonical composition verifier rejects extra chains/rules or base-chain metadata
drift. It then requires the exact allocated address on the same UP link identity,
an uncached IPv4
.B resolvectl
query bound to
.BR podlaz0 ,
a separate normal resolver lookup, and a TUN route for at least one returned
IPv4 address.
.B Current Scopes
is derived resolver runtime state and is not an acceptance criterion.
.PP
A committed transaction is historical proof that the transaction completed; it
is not permanent proof that the current host network still matches the verified
generation. The daemon therefore publishes current TUN health independently as
.BR verified ,
.BR revalidating ,
.BR degraded ,
or
.BR cleanup-required ,
with a positive
.B network_generation
and stable classification when applicable. Generation 1 is published as
.B verified
only after a fresh post-commit observation has passed canonical composition and
layered connectivity verification for that exact observation.
.PP
.B revalidating
and
.B degraded
may be transient active publications while proof is in progress or a terminal
verification outcome is being handed off. A proved verification failure or
revalidation deadline is not a stable active degraded state. The daemon keeps
old health evidence invalid, releases revalidation authority, captures bounded
redacted diagnostics before cleanup, and invokes the normal bounded lifecycle
.BR Disconnect .
Successful cleanup converges to inactive state and finalizes the saved report as
.BR completed .
Failed rollback finalizes it as
.B failed
and leaves the surviving owned state
.B cleanup-required
for recovery.
.PP
The daemon subscribes to systemd-logind post-resume notification and rtnetlink
link/address/route changes. Event payloads are hints, not state of record. A
fresh snapshot derives the underlying-uplink fingerprint from the default-route
interface and ifindex, gateway, relevant global IPv4 addresses, authoritative
NetworkManager active-connection identity when NetworkManager is present, and
the VPN-server bypass next hop. Podlaz-owned TUN, policy-routing, DNS and
nftables state is excluded so self-generated events cannot advance the network
generation. Failure to inspect NetworkManager active connections while
NetworkManager itself is detected is unknown evidence, not authoritative
absence.
.PP
Resume and watcher resubscription force a same-generation reproof even when the
fingerprint is unchanged. Each logind or rtnetlink source follows a
subscribe-then-resync contract: after a successful initial subscription or
reconnection it queues one coalesced fresh-snapshot revalidation, covering edges
that may have occurred while the source was unavailable. Link/address/route
storms remain bounded and coalesced.
.PP
Connect, disconnect and recovery retain mutation priority over current-health
probes. Mutation intent cancels any in-flight revalidation before waiting for the
shared lifecycle boundary. A trigger already consumed while a lifecycle mutation
is pending waits for mutation-idle rather than being discarded; an in-flight
trigger interrupted by mutation is requeued. After mutation, the retained hint
causes one fresh authoritative snapshot and normal fingerprint decision. Daemon
shutdown is terminal cancellation for event processing and does not requeue
work. Cancellation caused by explicit disconnect/recovery or shutdown is not
converted into terminal verification failure and does not start duplicate
cleanup.
.PP
If
.B network-apply,
.B network-verify,
connect-time connectivity verification, or proved post-commit revalidation
fails or reaches its terminal deadline, the daemon runs a short bounded redacted
diagnostic subset while the relevant failed state still exists. For terminal
revalidation this happens only after revalidation authority is released and
before the first automatic rollback command. The report records a stable primary
classification,
.B failure_phase,
and
.B rollback_status.
Known network lifecycle failures use
.B network_apply_failure
or
.BR network_verify_failure ;
overall status words such as
.B unhealthy
are never used as classifications. Address-specific failures use
.BR tun_address_conflict ,
.BR tun_address_apply_failure ,
or
.BR tun_address_verify_failure ;
layered revalidation retains the concrete failed verification phase and
.B revalidation_timeout
for a terminal deadline. The report is saved with rollback status
.B pending
and finalized atomically to
.B completed
or
.BR failed .
Diagnostic collection and persistence are best-effort and cannot suppress or
delay the separate bounded rollback context. A failed automatic rollback leaves
current health
.B cleanup-required
and preserves durable exact ownership for recovery.
.PP
During shutdown, the daemon first cancels network-event delivery and active
revalidation, then waits for the shared lifecycle mutation boundary. Restart
intent preserves the current-boot continuation while the normal exact
transaction-backed disconnect orders host-network rollback and Xray shutdown.
Explicit stop intent removes continuation before teardown begins. Any teardown
failure is returned from podlazd instead of being converted into a successful
clean shutdown, and exact transaction evidence is retained for later recovery.
The HTTP server is shut down within the same bounded shutdown budget, filesystem
socket paths are removed, listeners are closed, and the advisory lock is
released when its file descriptor closes; the inert lock file itself need not be
unlinked.
.PP
On disconnect, the daemon rolls back podlaz-owned host networking state, stops
the active Xray child, removes the generated runtime config path, and reports
inactive state. If Xray exits unexpectedly, the daemon keeps transaction state
for inspection or recovery and reports the core failure through status warnings.
.PP
Recovery dry-run inspection is read-only. Explicit recovery execution is scoped
to clearly podlaz-owned volatile resources and must skip ambiguous targets.
For the validated podlaz-owned
.B podlaz0
DNS target, missing-link cleanup is successful only for a normal
.B resolvectl
exit status 1 with empty stdout and the exact bounded supported
.B No such device
stderr. Caller cancellation or deadline, launch failure, signal termination,
permission denial, another exit code, unrelated exit status 1, unexpected
stdout, or different/unbounded stderr remains a failure. The exact Ubuntu 24.04
line containing
.BR ", ignoring: No such device"
is supported under the same strict envelope. This rule is shared by
direct stale-link cleanup and persisted transaction DNS rollback.
.PP
After connect, disconnect, rollback, or recovery, the daemon performs a bounded
authoritative refresh of its startup-scan publication. A newer successful scan
replaces older candidates; timeout or failure publishes incomplete/unknown
status and must not produce top-level recovery success. Cleanup-complete terminal transaction history may
remain visible but is non-blocking. A cleanup-required transaction or stale scan
candidate must not reject an immediate subsequent TUN connect. The runtime root
.I /run/podlaz
is not removed wholesale.
.SH LOGS
The packaged service writes daemon operational events to journald:
.EX
journalctl -u podlazd -n 50 --no-pager
journalctl -u podlazd -f
.EE
.PP
The user-facing command
.BR "podlaz logs"
uses
.BR journalctl (1)
to read recent daemon logs, and
.BR "podlaz logs --core"
filters structural Xray lifecycle and child-output-observed events. Raw Xray
stdout/stderr payloads, profile identifiers, endpoints, and arbitrary child text
are not persisted to journald.
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
.TP
.B Daemon is not reachable
Check service state and recent logs:
.EX
systemctl status podlazd --no-pager
journalctl -u podlazd -n 50 --no-pager
.EE
.TP
.B Permission denied or authentication service unavailable when using podlaz status or doctor
The normal packaged path is filesystem-socket attempt, permission-denied
fallback to the polkit-gated abstract socket, then daemon-side polkit
authorization for privileged operations. Verify the service is running in
packaged polkit mode and that polkit is installed and active:
.EX
systemctl status podlazd --no-pager
systemctl show podlazd -p Environment --no-pager
systemctl status polkit --no-pager || systemctl status polkitd --no-pager
journalctl -u podlazd -n 100 --no-pager
podlaz doctor
.EE
The service environment should include
.B PODLAZ_SERVICE=systemd
and
.BR PODLAZ_POLKIT_AUTHORIZATION=required .
.TP
.B Socket path exists but daemon cannot start
The daemon removes stale Unix sockets, but it fails when the socket path is a
non-socket file. Inspect and remove only the unexpected file after verifying it
is not owned by a running daemon.
.TP
.B Xray exited unexpectedly
Use:
.EX
podlaz status
podlaz logs --core
journalctl -u podlazd -n 100 --no-pager
.EE
.TP
.B Failed TUN connect or terminal revalidation with a saved diagnostic report
Use the canonical read-only diagnostic command:
.EX
podlaz doctor --tun --verbose
.EE
The historical report identifies the stable lifecycle or verification phase,
classification, and final rollback status without exposing raw profile secrets
or generated configuration.
.TP
.B Active TUN is committed but current health is not verified
This can be a transient publication while a generation is being reproved. A
proved verification failure or deadline does not remain active indefinitely as
.B degraded;
podlazd captures bounded redacted diagnostics and automatically attempts normal
bounded disconnect. If cleanup succeeds, status becomes inactive. If cleanup
fails, inspect the
.B cleanup-required
state and the saved report with
.B podlaz status
and
.B podlaz doctor --tun --verbose
before running recovery. Explicit user disconnect/recovery or shutdown
cancellation does not trigger a second automatic disconnect. Do not treat
revalidation failure as authority to repair or delete foreign or ambiguous
state.
.TP
.B Stale podlaz-owned runtime state
Inspect first:
.EX
podlaz recover
.EE
Then use explicit daemon-owned cleanup only when the output is understood:
.EX
podlaz recover --execute --yes
.EE
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Manual development install of service identities and the systemd unit:
.EX
sudo install -m 0644 packaging/sysusers.d/podlaz.conf \
  /usr/lib/sysusers.d/podlaz.conf
sudo systemd-sysusers /usr/lib/sysusers.d/podlaz.conf
sudo install -m 0644 packaging/systemd/podlazd.service \
  /etc/systemd/system/podlazd.service
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemd-analyze verify /etc/systemd/system/podlazd.service
sudo systemctl start podlazd
podlaz status
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR podlaz (1),
.BR systemd.service (5),
.BR journalctl (1),
